# Infrastructure as Code — {{projectName}}

Deploys {{projectName}} to **AWS EKS** with **Terraform** (infrastructure) and
**Helm** (the application). Everything is environment-scoped and driven by
`dude iac …`, which works identically on your laptop and in CI/CD.

```
iac/
├── terraform/
│   ├── versions.tf            # provider + S3 backend (partial)
│   ├── providers.tf           # aws / kubernetes / helm (exec auth)
│   ├── variables.tf           # all inputs (sane defaults)
│   ├── main.tf                # VPC + EKS + ALB controller{{#if withPostgres}} + RDS{{/if}} (reads shared ECR)
│   ├── outputs.tf             # cluster name, ECR URLs{{#if withPostgres}}, RDS endpoint{{/if}}
│   ├── bootstrap/             # one-time shared infra: S3 state bucket + DynamoDB lock + ECR repos
│   └── environments/
│       └── dev/
│           ├── backend.hcl        # remote-state location for this env
│           └── terraform.tfvars   # this env's sizing/knobs
└── helm/
    └── app/                   # the application chart (backend, frontend,{{#if withRedis}} redis,{{/if}}{{#if withCelery}} celery,{{/if}} ingress…)
```

## What gets provisioned

- **VPC** across 3 AZs (public + private{{#if withPostgres}} + database{{/if}} subnets), NAT gateway.
- **EKS** cluster + a managed node group.
- **ECR** repositories for the backend and frontend images — **shared** across
  all environments (created by `bootstrap`), so one image is promoted dev →
  staging → prod by tag rather than rebuilt per env.
- **AWS Load Balancer Controller** (for ALB Ingress), wired via IRSA.
{{#if withPostgres}}- **RDS PostgreSQL** (managed); the master password is generated by Terraform and exposed as the sensitive `database_url` output — **no AWS Secrets Manager**.
{{/if}}- **Redis**{{#if withRedis}} runs **in-cluster** via the Helm chart{{else}} is not used{{/if}}.

The application itself (backend, frontend,{{#if withCelery}} Celery worker{{#if withCeleryBeat}}, beat{{/if}}, Flower,{{/if}}{{#if withRedis}} Redis,{{/if}} Ingress) is deployed by the Helm chart.

## Prerequisites

- **Docker** running, plus the `aws` CLI on your PATH.
- AWS credentials configured (`aws configure` or environment variables / SSO).

You do **not** need terraform, kubectl, helm or k9s installed — `dude iac` runs
them inside a container (see below). The `aws` CLI is still needed on the host
because `dude iac login` opens a browser for SSO.

## The IaC runner (Docker)

`dude iac *` runs terraform / kubectl / helm / k9s inside a pinned container so
you don't have to install or version-match any of them locally. The image is
built on first use from `iac/runner/Dockerfile` — **which is yours to edit**
(bump a tool version, add a CLI). The image is tagged by a hash of that file, so
any change rebuilds automatically on the next command.

- **Credentials** are never copied in: your `~/.aws` is mounted read-only and
  the environment's `AWS_PROFILE` is passed through, so named profiles and SSO
  keep working. `~/.kube` is mounted too, so `kubeconfig`/`status`/`deploy`
  share the cluster context.
- **`dude iac login`** is the one command that stays on the host (it needs a
  browser for SSO); it writes the SSO token the container then reads.
- **`dude iac build`/`push`** use the host Docker daemon (they build app images);
  everything else runs in the container.
- **Escape hatch**: set `DUDE_IAC_RUNNER=host` to run the tools natively from
  your PATH instead (the legacy behaviour). If Docker isn't running, `dude iac`
  falls back to native tools automatically.
- **`dude iac shell --env <env>`** drops you into the runner with all tools +
  k9s, the env's profile and kubeconfig already wired — handy for ad-hoc
  inspection or changes.

## Credentials per environment

Each `dude iac …` command is environment-scoped via `--env`, which is
**required** — there is no default, so you never act on the wrong environment by
accident. Run any command without it to list the environments discovered under
`iac/terraform/environments/`. Each env resolves an **AWS profile**, so different
environments can point at different accounts/keys. The profile precedence:

1. an explicit `--profile <name>` flag,
2. an already-exported `AWS_PROFILE`,
3. the convention `{{projectName}}-<env>` (e.g. `{{projectName}}-dev`, `{{projectName}}-prod`).

`dude iac login` configures/verifies that profile for you:

```bash
dude iac login --env dev    # configures {{projectName}}-dev if missing, else verifies it
```

- If the profile doesn't exist, it runs `aws configure --profile {{projectName}}-<env>`.
- If it's an SSO profile, it runs `aws sso login --profile {{projectName}}-<env>`.
- Either way it ends with `aws sts get-caller-identity` to confirm you're in the
  right account.

Once logged in, every other command targets the same profile automatically — no
need to `export AWS_PROFILE` by hand.

## One-time: shared infra (state backend + ECR)

The Terraform state (S3 bucket + DynamoDB lock) and the ECR repositories are
shared by every environment. Create them and wire the backend into `backend.hcl`
in one step:

```bash
dude iac bootstrap --state-bucket-prefix <your-org> --region eu-west-1 --env dev --yes
```

This runs `terraform init/apply` inside `iac/terraform/bootstrap/` (local
state) — creating the state bucket, lock table, and the `{{projectName}}-backend`
/ `{{projectName}}-frontend` ECR repos — then writes the resulting bucket and
table names directly into `iac/terraform/environments/dev/backend.hcl`. No manual
copy-paste needed. Per-env Terraform then reads the ECR repos by name, so the
bootstrap must run before the first `apply`.

## Provision an environment

```bash
dude iac login     --env dev      # configure/verify AWS credentials for this env
dude iac bootstrap --state-bucket-prefix <your-org> --env dev --yes   # one-time
dude iac init      --env dev      # terraform init (configures the S3 backend)
dude iac plan      --env dev      # review changes
dude iac apply     --env dev      # create/update infrastructure
dude iac output    --env dev      # cluster name, ECR URLs{{#if withPostgres}}, RDS endpoint{{/if}}
```

## Build, push & deploy

`dude` drives the whole release. The ECR registry is read from Terraform
outputs and the image tag defaults to the **git short SHA** (with a `-dirty`
suffix if you have uncommitted changes), so every deploy is traceable to an
exact commit. Override with `--tag <tag>` (e.g. `--tag v1.2.3`).

The fast inner loop — build, push, and deploy in one step:

```bash
dude iac kubeconfig --env dev    # once per cluster: point kubectl/helm at it
dude iac ship       --env dev    # build → push → helm upgrade --install
dude iac status     --env dev    # release status + pods
```

Or run the stages individually (handy in CI, where each is a separate stage):

```bash
dude iac build  --env dev        # docker build BE+FE (linux/amd64) for the ECR repos
dude iac push   --env dev        # ECR login + docker push BE+FE
dude iac deploy --env dev        # helm upgrade --install (auto-sets registry + tag)
```

> **Platform** — images are built for `linux/amd64` by default to match the EKS
> x86 nodes. Building on Apple Silicon without this flag yields arm64 images that
> crash on the cluster with `exec format error`. Override with `--platform` if
> your node group is arm64 (Graviton).

`deploy` injects `image.registry` and the image tags via `--set`, so you do not
hand-edit them into `values-dev.yaml` — that file is still layered for the rest
(resources, replicas{{#if withPostgres}}, secrets{{/if}}…).

> **Config & secrets** — every app container loads its env via `envFrom` from a
> ConfigMap (`.Values.config`) and a Secret (`.Values.secrets`). Each key is the
> ENV VAR NAME exactly as `app/core/config.py` reads it, so adding a variable is
> one line under `config:` / `secrets:` plus the field in `config.py`. Per-env
> `values-<env>.yaml` files are **gitignored** (they may hold secrets like API
> keys); the committed `values.yaml` is the documented reference.

{{#if withPostgres}}`secrets.DATABASE_URL` must be present (the chart refuses to deploy otherwise),
but you don't set it by hand: `dude iac ship`/`deploy` injects it via
`--set-string` from the Terraform `database_url` output (`dude iac output database_url`).

{{/if}}Find the public URL:

```bash
kubectl get ingress -n dev
```

## HTTPS / TLS (custom domain)

By default the app is served over plain **HTTP** via the ALB's own DNS name.
The ALB hostname (`*.elb.amazonaws.com`) belongs to AWS, so you can't put a
browser-trusted certificate on it — TLS requires **a domain you own**.

If your domain's hosted zone is in **Route53**, it's fully automated. Set two
variables in `iac/terraform/environments/<env>/terraform.tfvars`:

```hcl
domain_name       = "dev.example.com"   # the hostname for this environment
route53_zone_name = "example.com"        # the existing Route53 hosted zone
```

Then re-apply and re-deploy:

```bash
dude iac apply  --env dev    # issues an ACM cert (DNS-validated) + runs external-dns
dude iac deploy --env dev    # wires the cert ARN + host into the ALB Ingress
```

What happens under the hood:

- Terraform issues a **regional ACM certificate** for `domain_name` and validates
  it automatically by writing the DNS records into your Route53 zone.
- Terraform installs **external-dns**, which watches the Ingress and creates the
  Route53 record pointing `domain_name` at the ALB (it runs `upsert-only`, so it
  never deletes records).
- `dude iac deploy` passes the cert ARN and host to Helm, so the ALB listens on
  **443** with the cert and **redirects HTTP→HTTPS**.

The certificate is regional and lives in `var.region` (same region as the ALB).
DNS propagation + cert validation take a couple of minutes on first apply.

> **DNS elsewhere (not Route53)?** Keep `route53_zone_name` empty isn't an option —
> instead create the ACM cert manually (or import the zone), add the validation
> CNAME at your provider, point a CNAME at the ALB by hand, and set
> `ingress.certificateArn` / `ingress.host` in `values-<env>.yaml`. The Route53
> path above is the only fully-automated one.

## Add a new environment

```bash
dude iac new-env --env prod          # copies dev by default; use --from <env> to pick the source
```

This scaffolds `iac/terraform/environments/prod/{backend.hcl,terraform.tfvars}`,
setting `environment = "prod"` and a per-env state `key` (the S3 bucket + lock
table **and the ECR repos** are **shared** across environments, so you don't
bootstrap again unless this is the first env on a brand-new backend). It also
copies the source env's gitignored `iac/helm/app/values-<from>.yaml` to
`values-prod.yaml` when present, so the new env starts from the same Helm
overrides + secrets instead of the bare `values.yaml` defaults. After
scaffolding, run `dude iac init --env prod` then `apply`.

Then edit `environments/prod/terraform.tfvars` (bigger nodes,{{#if withPostgres}} `db_multi_az = true`, `db_deletion_protection = true`,{{/if}} domain, …)
and `iac/helm/app/values-prod.yaml` (replicas, autoscaling, secrets, …), and run
every command with `--env prod`:

```bash
dude iac init --env prod && dude iac apply --env prod && dude iac ship --env prod
```

## Local vs CI/CD

The exact same commands run in CI — state is remote (S3) and auth is via the
AWS CLI exec plugin, so no kubeconfig file is committed. A pipeline typically
runs: `dude iac init/plan/apply` → `dude iac build` → `dude iac push` →
`dude iac deploy` (or `dude iac ship` to collapse the last three). Pass an
explicit `--tag $CI_COMMIT_SHA` so the pipeline controls provenance.

## Tear down

```bash
dude iac destroy --env dev
```

`destroy` tears down **everything** for the environment, in order:

1. uninstalls the Helm release;
2. deletes any load balancers, target groups and managed security groups still
   tagged for the cluster (these are created by the in-cluster controller, not
   Terraform, and otherwise block the VPC/subnet teardown);
3. removes the Route53 records external-dns created for this env (it runs
   upsert-only and never deletes them itself, so they would otherwise outlive
   the ALB they point at and block the next deploy from reusing the hostname);
4. `terraform destroy` of the environment infrastructure (this never touches the
   shared ECR repos — they live in the bootstrap state, not the per-env one);
5. tears down the bootstrap — the shared state backend (S3 bucket + DynamoDB lock
   table) **and** the shared ECR repos — but **only if no other environment still
   uses it** (see the note below).

Flags for finer control:

| Flag             | Effect                                                              |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `--keep-backend` | Keep the shared bootstrap (S3/DynamoDB state backend **+ ECR repos**). It is kept automatically while another environment still uses it; pass this to force-keep it even when destroying the last environment. |
| `--skip-helm`    | Skip the Helm uninstall (the release is already gone). LB cleanup still runs. |
| `--skip-tf`      | Skip the per-env `terraform destroy` — only run cleanup + backend teardown. |

> The bootstrap holds the project-wide shared resources — the state backend (S3
> bucket + DynamoDB lock table) **and** the ECR repos — used by every environment
> (they differ only by their state `key`). To protect the other environments'
> state *and registry*, `destroy` automatically **keeps** the bootstrap whenever
> another environment still points at the same bucket, and only tears it down
> when you're destroying the **last** environment. Pass `--keep-backend` to
> force-keep it even then. (A per-env `destroy` never deletes the shared ECR —
> the repos aren't in the per-env state.)

## Commands

| Command | Wraps |
|---------|-------|
| `dude iac login`     | `aws configure` / `aws sso login` + `sts get-caller-identity` |
| `dude iac new-env`   | scaffolds `environments/<name>/` from an existing env (`--env <name>`, `--from <src>`) |
| `dude iac bootstrap` | `terraform init/apply` in `bootstrap/`, patches `backend.hcl` |
| `dude iac init`      | `terraform init` (S3 backend per env) |
| `dude iac plan`      | `terraform plan` |
| `dude iac apply`     | `terraform apply` (`--yes` to auto-approve) |
| `dude iac destroy`   | helm uninstall + ALB/SG cleanup + `terraform destroy` + backend teardown (`--yes`, `--keep-backend`, `--skip-helm`, `--skip-tf`) |
| `dude iac output`    | `terraform output` (`--json`) |
| `dude iac fmt`       | `terraform fmt -recursive` |
| `dude iac validate`  | `terraform validate` |
| `dude iac build`     | `docker build` BE+FE prod images (`linux/amd64`) |
| `dude iac push`      | ECR login + `docker push` BE+FE |
| `dude iac kubeconfig`| `aws eks update-kubeconfig` from outputs |
| `dude iac deploy`    | `helm upgrade --install` (auto-sets registry + tag + TLS cert/host) |
| `dude iac ship`      | `build` + `push` + `deploy` |
| `dude iac status`    | `helm status` + `kubectl get pods` |
| `dude iac shell`     | interactive shell in the IaC runner container (terraform, kubectl, helm, k9s, aws) |

All commands take `--env <name>` (**required**; autodiscovered from
`environments/*`) and `--profile <name>` (AWS profile override; defaults to
`{{projectName}}-<env>` or `$AWS_PROFILE`).
`build`/`push`/`deploy`/`ship` take `--tag <tag>` (default: git short SHA,
`-dirty` if uncommitted); `build`/`ship` also take `--platform` (default
`linux/amd64`). `bootstrap` accepts `--state-bucket-prefix` (required) and
`--region` (default `eu-west-1`).
